</figure>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ B.3 Earth and Climate Science Datasets on Spheres ‣ Appendix B More Experimental Results ‣ Kuramoto Orientation Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> visualizes the learned densities produced by our method, capturing both highly concentrated regions (<em id="A2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> volcanic and fire clusters) and dispersed patterns. Table <a href="#A2.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ B.3 Earth and Climate Science Datasets on Spheres ‣ Appendix B More Experimental Results ‣ Kuramoto Orientation Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> presents the test negative log-likelihood (NLL) on each dataset. Our method achieves comparable performance against these baselines. We compute NLL using the change-of-variables formula under the time-reversed ODE solver: <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p({\bm{\theta}}_{0})=\log p({\bm{\theta}}_{T})+\sum_{t=T}^{1}\text{Tr}(\mathcal{J}_{{\bm{b}}({\bm{\theta}}_{t},t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p({\bm{\theta}}_{0})=\log p({\bm{\theta}}_{T})+\sum_{t=T}^{1}\text{Tr}(\mathcal{J}_{{\bm{b}}({\bm{\theta}}_{t},t)})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{\theta}}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{\theta}}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the von Mises prior and <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}({\bm{\theta}}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}({\bm{\theta}}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the backward drift, <em id="A2.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}({\bm{\theta}}_{t},t)=-{\bm{f}}({\bm{\theta}}_{t},t)+D_{t}\cdot s({\bm{\theta}}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}({\bm{\theta}}_{t},t)=-{\bm{f}}({\bm{\theta}}_{t},t)+D_{t}\cdot s({\bm{\theta}}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>. The Jacobian trace is estimated via Hutchinson’s stochastic estimator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While our method achieves competitive NLL scores across datasets, we note that direct comparison across models can be influenced by the choice of different priors. In particular, von Mises distributions with higher concentration can artificially boost log-likelihood scores, whereas broader priors can deflate them. Thus, while our results confirm the effectiveness of our model, qualitative evaluations remain critical for comprehensive assessments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Navier-Stokes Fluid Velocity Field</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate the applicability to real angular data, we evaluate our Kuramoto diffusion model on 2D incompressible Navier–Stokes (NS) velocity fields from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Each velocity frame <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{v}}_{x},{\bm{v}}_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{v}}_{x},{\bm{v}}_{y})</annotation></semantics></math> is converted to polar form:</p>
<table id="A2.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{r}}=\sqrt{{\bm{v}}_{x}^{2}+{\bm{v}}_{y}^{2}},\quad{\bm{\theta}}=\texttt{arctan2}({\bm{v}}_{y},{\bm{v}}_{x})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒓</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">arctan2</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{r}}=\sqrt{{\bm{v}}_{x}^{2}+{\bm{v}}_{y}^{2}},\quad{\bm{\theta}}=\texttt{arctan2}({\bm{v}}_{y},{\bm{v}}_{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{r}}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒓</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{r}}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is the amplitude and <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{\theta}}\in(-\pi,\pi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\theta}}\in(-\pi,\pi]</annotation></semantics></math> is the phase. Since <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive, we work in log–magnitude
<math id="A2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}=\log{\bm{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝒓</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}=\log{\bm{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> and apply a VP SDE there in the log space, while phases evolve via the locally coupled Kuramoto SDE. The forward processes are defined as:</p>
<table id="A2.E21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A2.E21X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E21X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\texttt{Amplitude:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Amplitude:</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\texttt{Amplitude:}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E21X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\mathop{d{\bm{z}}_{t}^{i}}}{\mathop{dt}}=-\frac{1}{2}\beta_{t}{\bm{z}}_{t}^{i}+\sqrt{\beta_{t}}\xi^{i},\quad{\bm{z}}_{0}=\log{{\bm{r}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝒓</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\mathop{d{\bm{z}}_{t}^{i}}}{\mathop{dt}}=-\frac{1}{2}\beta_{t}{\bm{z}}_{t}^{i}+\sqrt{\beta_{t}}\xi^{i},\quad{\bm{z}}_{0}=\log{{\bm{r}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E21Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E21Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\texttt{Phase:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Phase:</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\texttt{Phase:}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E21Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d{\bm{\theta}}^{i}_{t}}{dt}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{N}_{i}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}}K(t)\sin({\bm{\theta}}^{j}_{t}-{\bm{\theta}}^{i}_{t})+K_{\text{ref}}(t)\sin(\psi_{\text{ref}}-{\bm{\theta}}^{i}_{t})+\sqrt{2D_{t}}\xi^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d{\bm{\theta}}^{i}_{t}}{dt}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{N}_{i}|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}}K(t)\sin({\bm{\theta}}^{j}_{t}-{\bm{\theta}}^{i}_{t})+K_{\text{ref}}(t)\sin(\psi_{\text{ref}}-{\bm{\theta}}^{i}_{t})+\sqrt{2D_{t}}\xi^{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In the reverse process we train two score models, one in log–magnitude space and one on phases, to solve the corresponding reverse SDEs and jointly synthesize <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{r}},{\bm{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒓</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{r}},{\bm{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following fluid–dynamics practice, we assess realism in the spectral domain at the final time step. From each 2D velocity field we compute the radial energy spectrum <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(k)</annotation></semantics></math> and then: (i) fit a line to
<math id="A2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log E(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log E(k)</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log k</annotation></semantics></math> over the mid–wavenumber band <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[0.1,0.4]\cdot k_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[0.1,0.4]\cdot k_{max}</annotation></semantics></math> (with <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{max}=\min(H,W)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{max}=\min(H,W)/2</annotation></semantics></math>) and report the absolute slope difference, and (ii) compute the 1D Wasserstein distance between the <em id="A2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normalized</em> mean spectra of real and generated samples.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>Spectral evaluation of generated Navier-Stokes fluid velocity fields.</figcaption>
<div id="A2.T9.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:341.6pt;height:28.7pt;vertical-align:-12.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-50.2pt,4.2pt) scale(0.772983450640583,0.772983450640583) ;">
<table id="A2.T9.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A2.T9.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T9.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">Metrics</th>
<th id="A2.T9.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">Slope Difference (<math id="A2.T9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<th id="A2.T9.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Wasserstein Distance (<math id="A2.T9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.T9.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T9.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">SGM (VP-SDE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite></th>
<td id="A2.T9.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.7435</td>
<td id="A2.T9.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.0015</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T9.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">
<th id="A2.T9.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="A2.T9.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">Ours (Kuramoto Phase + Log-VP Amplitude)</span></th>
<td id="A2.T9.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="A2.T9.2.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">0.6954</span></td>
<td id="A2.T9.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A2.T9.2.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">0.0011</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ B.4 Navier-Stokes Fluid Velocity Field ‣ Appendix B More Experimental Results ‣ Kuramoto Orientation Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows that coupling phases through the local Kuramoto dynamics while diffusing the log–magnitude yields spectra closer to the true data: slope error is reduced by <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> and Wasserstein distance is decreased by <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="27\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">27\%</annotation></semantics></math> relative to a standard diffusion model that treats angle and magnitude independently. This improvement suggests that the non-isotropic and non-linear Kuramoto-based angular coupling can improve physical plausibility in vector–valued generative modeling, demonstrating the benefit of synchronization-based inductive biases in real angular data modeling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.5 </span>More Examples of Generative Samples</h3>

<div id="A2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figs. <a href="#A2.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ B.5 More Examples of Generative Samples ‣ Appendix B More Experimental Results ‣ Kuramoto Orientation Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, <a href="#A2.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ B.5 More Examples of Generative Samples ‣ Appendix B More Experimental Results ‣ Kuramoto Orientation Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>,  <a href="#A2.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ B.5 More Examples of Generative Samples ‣ Appendix B More Experimental Results ‣ Kuramoto Orientation Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, and <a href="#A2.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ B.5 More Examples of Generative Samples ‣ Appendix B More Experimental Results ‣ Kuramoto Orientation Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> present additional randomly generated samples from our 1000-step locally coupled Kuramoto orientation diffusion model on the SOCOFing fingerprint, Brodatz texture, ground terrain, and CIFAR10 datasets, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15328v1/finger_app.png" id="A2.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:471/589;" width="471" height="589" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>Randomly generated samples on SOCOFing fingerprint dataset.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F11" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15328v1/tex_app.png" id="A2.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:381/477;" width="381" height="477" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span>Randomly generated samples on Brodatz textures dataset.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F12" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15328v1/terrain_app.png" id="A2.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:471/588;" width="471" height="588" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12: </span>Randomly generated samples on the ground terrain dataset.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F13" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15328v1/cifar_app.png" id="A2.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:381/477;" width="381" height="477" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>Randomly generated samples on CIFAR10 dataset.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
